k-yous and thank yous for  subscribing so trigger communications  have become a vital part in the workflow  because they are preset automated  messages that you create one time and  then you set them to be sent  automatically which saves you time and  money of paying other people to do this  by hand along with better systems as  better data we can understand more about  who our recipients are we can ask for  more information about them we can go to  a third party and supplement the  information that we have about them we  can look at where they are regionally we  can also look at demographic information  we can also look at their tendency to  open emails and click through emails to  see if they're usually on a mobile or if  they're usually on a desktop or if it's  a combination we can learn much more  about our recipients and speak to them  in ways that they want to listen and so  the better we personalize and understand  our recipients the better messages we  can send them one of the greatest things  that has enabled email is the ability to  track email we can look at all of the  links that we send out in an email  message and we can see who clicks on  those links where they go what they do  and we can come up with a valuation of  the profitability of each campaign that  we do and so before you send out the  next campaign you can always go back and  see what worked and what did not work  what did people respond  - what did they not respond to you can  even send to a small test set of names  to see how they respond to test your  email and then adjust it before you send  to the larger group tracking has become  much more proliferates so that we can  see immediately the success of a  campaign and the factors that led to  that success  segmentation enables us to look at our  list in groups rather than as a big  batch of names we can start to segment  out based on behavior what people have  done what they've seen what they  downloaded what they purchased or you  can find out more about your users  simply by asking them and you can find  out their gender their age their birth  date their zip code and so then you can  start to segment by region depending  upon your business and you can send  different messages to different people  from a business standpoint you can email  people differently who live in northern  colder climates than people who live in  southern warmer climates and you can  market to them differently based on the  type of business you are if that's  relevant to you and see differences in  the way people behave just based on  region and when you segment out what  you're doing is taking groups of like  people and treating them differently  based on who they are and when you do  that you move into personalization it's  very easy now just to take the names and  the data that you have in your list and  personalize each email that you send  with the recipients name maybe with  their birth date  or with any other information that you  have the bigger database that you create  about your users the more you'll have an  ability to personalize their message and  the more it's personalized the more  people will react to that you have a  higher open rate a higher quick rate a  higher transaction rate the more  personal your emails become let's look  at how this all comes together with an  example you see it's a numbers game it  starts with the list  as we talked about the most valuable  most profitable list you'll probably  ever build is the one that you develop  yourself what times you may need to go  out and find a list especially if you're  just starting out or getting into a  different area and so it starts with the  list the number of names that you have  to start with the next step is to get  into the inbox there's a lot of  obstacles here because of the  proliferation of spam Internet service  providers have enacted many ways of  filtering email marketing messages if  your email looks too spammy or kicks off  on the filter it won't make it to the  Inbox  and then again you also have at the user  level sometimes people have spam filters  in addition to what their internet  service provider has and so you've got  to get through those filters to get into  the inbox once your email gets into the  inbox you're competing with all the  other emails that a person has received  that day or at that time and so the next  step is to get the user to open your  email and then to click through and as  you can see you're losing more and more  of those names to get to what you want  people to do and so getting people to  open the email is one thing getting them  to click through the offer to find out  more information is another and then  once they see the information they have  to decide that it's relevant and timely  and a necessity for them right now to  get that conversion that conversion  could be a sale or it could be a lead in  this case we're going to make it a lead  because ultimately that lead has to  result in a sale and so we need so many  leads before we can get so many sales  but here's the final cap on the whole  thing and this goes back to one of the  new developments and that's measurement  measurement that is what is the lifetime  value of customers that were acquired  what is the lifetime value of the  loyalty email that we just sent out to  the specific segment what was the value  of that mailing you can measure every  mailing  that you send to every segment and you  can look at the value to know what was  your return on investment for developing  this email or this email campaign and  see immediately how you can adjust what  needs to be adjust or if you need to  change different aspects throughout this  entire measurement funnel the downside  is there's a lot of steps you've got to  look at a lot of different factors to  determine if you had the right message  to the right people at the right time  sending them to the right page with the  right offer in order to get them to  convert and become a lead or a sale but  ultimately you always need to measure  the danger is a lot of people tend to  focus on how many opens did I get or how  many clicks did I get and really what  you're measuring are two steps in the  middle of the funnel rather than  measuring the whole funnel itself  you can't just measure the parts you've  got to measure the entire campaign now  here's an example of a campaign and this  is with a purchase list of high quality  names and we know it's a high quality  list because of the response rate so in  this example the list cost about $5,000  to get 200,000 high-quality names now of  those 200,000 names only 140,000 landed  into an inbox so only about 70% of those  names got past spam filters or were  valid email addresses of that 140,000  only thirty-seven thousand 800 were  opened so we're now down to about 27% of  our names were opened then you'll see a  drastic difference between an open and a  click so the 200,000 names were only  down to about four percent sixteen  hundred and twenty-five names are still  in the frontal and click through to the  landing page of those 16 hundred and  twenty-five that got to the landing page  52 became ally  they converted and became a lead now of  those 5214 became a sail now that's a  conversion rate of 26% which is  fantastic that's great and of those 14  sales we know that there are an average  of four annual sales for every new  customer so if we have 14 new customers  and an average of four sales a year with  an average sale of about eighteen  hundred dollars that comes down to 54  new sales over the lifetime of those  customers that means the campaign  generated eighteen hundred and  forty-seven percent above that $5,000  initial investment for the list there  was a return on an investment of  eighteen hundred percent so as you can  see what it takes to develop an email  campaign is a good list a good offer and  a good conversion rate of getting people  from that lead into the sale and then  looking at your lifetime value helps you  to get a better perspective rather than  just the initial campaign but looking at  the long-term value of a customer that  was acquired through that campaign and  you can see that the return on  investment really makes the case that  email is a highly profitable activity in  terms of the entire marketing mix IBM  created a campaign very quickly with a  budget of $2,000 with that $2,000 they  created a personal trainer email  campaign that went out to IT  professionals for training and education  that $2,000 campaign resulted in  validated leads worth nearly five  million dollars the ROI of that campaign  is well beyond imagination how great  that would have been to have worked on  that campaign and see that return on  investment you see when you've got the  right names  and you bring the right message to the  right people at the right time with the  right offer that is the heart of  marketing and that's what email enables  us to do with a highly measurable and  high return on investment and that's why  email marketing is far from being dead  [Music]  hey once you become an expert in digital  marketing then subscribe to the simply  learned Channel and click here to watch  more such videos turn up and get  certified in digital marketing click  here

